 go through each package or  anything but just to highlight the ones  that have made just a tremendous  difference  in our efficiency  deep flyer of course for  general wrangling ggplot2 for  visualizations stringar for  text stuff per  tidyr fur  for  you know functional programming  lubridae to wrangle dates skimar to  continuously preview and assess the data  janitor for cleaning and quick frequency  tables  and many others that that we utilize  also on a regular basis um such as tidy  text for text mining  and some packages from from the open  source community like layla's package  called dope for drug parsing  for modeling  we  actually only make use of of tidy models  really because it's just a tremendous  ecosystem of packages that help you with  the modeling process really from  start to  finish  and we use  the tidy models ecosystem to perform  statistical inference  survival analysis  and prediction  and so  these allow us to detect  and assess trends  at the population level at the provider  level  at the individual patient level  it they allow us to evaluate practices  internally  which then help us inform  what those practices ought to be  so this this is hugely helpful in  personalizing care for our patients  and then of course the continuous  monitoring of outcomes  and  assessing who may be most at risk for  treatment lapses and and so on  and so one thing that jared has has  talked about um  is  you know this this common pitfall that  that many of us may encounter um in  which you know you open a project you  run the code and depending on  how computationally heavy that that  could be you know  minutes or hours  you wait uh for that code to run and  then you  you run into some errors and then you  have to go and fix them and then have to  restart this uh  from scratch  and so  we don't have to live this way  people have um created packages um for  us to do that and the targets package is  is extremely powerful in that in that  sense so it is a pipeline toolkit for  reproducibility  the amazing thing about it is although  there are many tools this one is  specifically designed for r so you can  stay in r the entire time  it saves on computation with parallel  computing skips steps that are already  up to date and don't need to be run  and then that diagram that jared shows  um  you know gives you confidence that that  what you're doing is is truly  reproducible and so given that you know  we are a shop that uses data science  we're also very invested in research and  so this is a huge  win in in that regard  and so one of the most important things  of course uh for us is is reporting  right um so the key considerations here  is that um i did want to stay all in r  or our studio  i wanted that reporting to be  customizable in which i could throw up  you know my company's logo  wanted it to be lightweight and and  portable so easily shareable and i  wanted of course version control  and so  uh there are  amazing packages to help and do that so  starting with blastula which allows  for you to send emails from r so so  that's really helpful for daily kpi  distribution key performance indicators  our markdown  and flex dashboard together to make  reports html reports or to make those  into  dashboards  and sharing in for presentations and so  these four  we use  every day for  kpi distributions  monthly quarterly yearly reports  dashboards and presentations that we can  make iteratively without having to  recode them every single time  and so that's all great you know making  making this but how do we actually  deliver it to  key stakeholders  and  the tool we use is our studio connect  which for the consideration we just  spoke about about wanting to stay all in  r in our studio  that goes obviously a huge way  and so our studio connect is is that  publishing platform for those dashboards  and those on-demand and scheduled  reports and those presentations and even  apis and and and many more  so the advantage is there once again is  that you you never have to leave r r  studio it also can publish python code  for you i've never made use of python  but maybe one day  and then the third and  possibly one of the biggest  considerations is security given that we  are dealing with  confidential  patient data and so you can  uh very easily control who sees what on  that on that platform  and so i just wanted to to end here with  you know sort of the motivation for why  we do this work and why we get up every  day  is is for patients like this who  continue to inspire and astonish all of  us  this patient says i just wanted to thank  you and everyone else that has gotten  me this far i didn't think it was going  to happen without me driving to  pennsylvania but thanks to you guys i  finally have a doctor appointment and i  really appreciate you all so that's what  we're all about we're about lowering  that  barrier  to  treatment access  and uh we're  we're very happy to have the tools that  allow us to do that every day so huge  shout out to the open source community  and our studio  and everyone that i got to know through  twitter couldn't couldn't make this  happen without you guys so  thank you and  if you do want to reach out  by email or twitter please feel free to  do so and i hope  that if you want to get involved there's  many opportunities to do so  both  code wise but but also community wise i  encourage everyone to  look into narcan training  which  is oftentimes free  and offered multiple times a year in  which  they teach you how to recognize the  signs of an overdose and then administer  a life-saving drug called narcan which  reverses overdoses so with that thanks  once again and looking forward to  connect with you all

